    "story": "Somebody offered him real money for this car yesterday — money with a deadline — and he told them to come back when the weather turned, because a man deserves to look at what he's selling in the rain. The rain has now turned. The buyer has not. The keys are in his pocket, and the garage door is half open, and the only thing standing between the car and dry is whether either of them means what he said.",
    "notes": "The rain is the portrait's co-author: beading on the hood and fenders, pooling on the pavement, turning a parked car into a still life that happens to have an engine. The close crop — grille, headlights, hood ornament, nothing else — is a portraitist's choice, the machine given the same treatment as a face, and the black and white handling strips away every distraction the color frames lean on. Among the Cars set it is the quietest image and the most exact: no roadside, no neon, no companion to lean on, just chrome and water, and the painterly touch keeps both from going pretty. Collectors take it for the restraint — a car photographed the way a still life is photographed, at the distance where age reads as patina rather than damage.",
