    "story": "The office light is on for the last time, and the clerk already knows it. The trucks in the lot belong to men who were told to be gone by morning, and the morning is coming whether the gravel is wet or not. Somebody is going to sleep in a wigwam tonight — just not the man who has slept in one every night for forty years.",
    "notes": "This is the set's wide shot, and it earns the last place: the whole establishment in one frame — sign, office, and a row of vehicles parked there longer than any of them was meant to be. The rusty pickup in the foreground speaks for the lot, patched and faded and still holding its place, while the neon overhead does the promising. Wayne Heim's painterly handling mutes the color until the scene reads like a memory of the place rather than a report on it, which is exactly how roadside motels survive — as the idea of themselves. Hung as the collection's closing image it does what a good last page does: it lets the viewer stand in the lot, look back at the sign, and wonder whether to stay.",
